{
  "dom_id": "step-q341491",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(-4,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X<-3)=0.6915,$  use the table to find ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ \n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6950\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-3)=0.6915\\,⇒\\,P(X\\le -3)=0.6915\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le -3) & =0.6915 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{-3-(-4)}{\\sigma }) & =0.6915 \\\\ Φ\\,(\\frac{1}{\\sigma }) & =0.6915 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.6915)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.50)=0.6915\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.6915)=0.50.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.50 \\\\ \\sigma  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
